<p><strong>The Shaw Home</strong></p>
<p><strong>411 6th Ave NW</strong></p>
<p>Mr. and Mrs. {% person "benjamin-whitworth-b-w-shaw" %}Benjamin W. Shaw{% endperson %}’s home was contracted and constructed by {% person "agaton-larson" %}Agaton Larson{% endperson %} in 1918-1919. They resided in this home for 55 of their 57 years in Mandan.</p>
<p>Benjamin, a young attorney from Wisconsin, first came to Hillsboro, North Dakota in 1881 at the age of 26. Benjamin moved to Mandan in 1883 after his friend, {% person "william-a-lanterman" %}Will Lanterman{% endperson %}, purchased the {% place "hager-lumber-yard" %}Hager Lumber Yard{% endplace %}. William’s sister, {% person "georgia-lanterman-shaw" %}Georgia{% endperson %}, later became Benjamin’s wife.</p>
<p>Benjamin began his law practice that same year by forming a partnership with {% person "f-e-bowen" %}F.E. Bowen{% endperson %}, City Attorney, whom he later succeeded. Benjamin’s wife, Georgia, was connected to many well known attorneys and partnered with Judge {% person "s-l-nuchols" %}S.L. Nuchols{% endperson %} for many years.</p>
<p>Benjamin was probably best known for as Morton County Judge, an elective position he held for 32 years. He was the president of the {% place "north-dakota-state-bar-association" %}State Bar Association{% endplace %} and, at one time, served as County Commissioner in the early days of Morton County. He also served in the {% place "north-dakota-house-of-representatives" %}House of Representatives{% endplace %} at various sessions as bill clerk, reading clerk, and assistant chief clerk.</p>
<p>Georgia passed away in 1939 and Benjamin in 1940. They had two children from their union: a son and one daughter, who remained residents of Mandan. The current residents of the home, Mr. A and {% person "paula-milan-olsen" %}Paula Milan Olsen{% endperson %}, purchased the home in 2004.</p>
